  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times its value so the bowl fits. It is steeper along x, so overshooting shows there first. Plain steps flip sign along x when the rate is over 0.4 and grow when it is over 0.8.",
    "Each step = -rate x (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread sqrt((64-b)/(b*63)) for batch size b: 1 at b=1 and exactly zero at 64. So a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate x slope), not slopes. They are drawn at 3 times their true length, the same scale for both. Mini-batch step is the move the ball makes; Full-data step is the move with no noise.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss goes over 5, the loss at the x rim (diverged). A path point outside the bowl is drawn at the rim. The Loss readout always shows the true value.",
    "The trail starts at the start point and adds one point per step. New run, keep last path keeps the finished run as the ghost and uses the next noise values. Reset replays the same fixed-seed noise from the start.",
    "Changing a setting during a run affects the next step."
  ]
}